1
00:00:01,293 --> 00:00:03,002
Your argument is lacking
in all scientific merit. 

2
00:00:03,044 --> 00:00:04,755
It is well established

3
00:00:04,797 --> 00:00:06,841
Superman cleans
his uniform

4
00:00:06,882 --> 00:00:08,425
by flying into
Earth's yellow sun,

5
00:00:08,467 --> 00:00:10,177
which incinerates any
contaminate matter

6
00:00:10,218 --> 00:00:11,553
and leaves the
invulnerable

7
00:00:11,595 --> 00:00:16,057
Kryptonian fabric unharmed
and daisy fresh.

8
00:00:16,099 --> 00:00:18,018
What if he gets something
Kryptonian on it?

9
00:00:18,059 --> 00:00:19,394
Like what?

10
00:00:19,436 --> 00:00:22,397
I don't know.
Kryptonian mustard.

11
00:00:22,439 --> 00:00:25,192
I think we can safely assume
that all Kryptonian condiments

12
00:00:25,233 --> 00:00:28,570
were destroyed when the
planet Krypton exploded.

13
00:00:28,612 --> 00:00:30,322
Or it turned into
mustard Kryptonite,

14
00:00:30,364 --> 00:00:31,657
the only way to destroy

15
00:00:31,699 --> 00:00:34,952
a rogue Kryptonian hotdog
threatening Earth.

16
00:00:34,994 --> 00:00:36,286
LEONARD:
Raj, please,

17
00:00:36,328 --> 00:00:38,539
let's stay serious here.

18
00:00:38,580 --> 00:00:40,290
Superman's body
is Kryptonian,

19
00:00:40,332 --> 00:00:41,834
therefore his sweat
is Kryptonian.

20
00:00:41,876 --> 00:00:44,837
Yeah, what about
Kryptonian pit stains?

21
00:00:44,879 --> 00:00:46,547
Superman doesn't
sweat on Earth.

22
00:00:46,588 --> 00:00:48,340
WOLOWITZ:
Okay,

23
00:00:48,382 --> 00:00:50,009
he's invited for dinner in
the Bottle City of Kandor.

24
00:00:50,049 --> 00:00:51,677
He miniaturizes himself,
enters the city

25
00:00:51,719 --> 00:00:52,845
where he loses his
superpowers. Now,

26
00:00:52,887 --> 00:00:54,513
before dinner,
his host says,

27
00:00:54,555 --> 00:00:56,807
"Who's up for a little
Kryptonian tetherball?"

28
00:00:56,849 --> 00:00:58,642
Superman says "Sure,"
works up a sweat,

29
00:00:58,684 --> 00:01:00,227
comes back to Earth,
his uniform now stained

30
00:01:00,268 --> 00:01:03,104
with indestructible
Kryptonian perspiration.

31
00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:07,735
Boo ya.

32
00:01:09,945 --> 00:01:11,405
Superman would have
taken his uniform

33
00:01:11,446 --> 00:01:13,991
to a Kandorian dry cleaner
before he left the Bottle.

34
00:01:15,409 --> 00:01:17,244
"Kandorian dry cl..." I give up.

35
00:01:17,285 --> 00:01:19,454
You can't have a rational
argument with this man.

36
00:01:20,998 --> 00:01:21,957
WOLOWITZ:
Hey,

37
00:01:21,999 --> 00:01:23,250
isn't that the guy who won

38
00:01:23,291 --> 00:01:24,960
the MacArthur
genius grant last year?

39
00:01:25,002 --> 00:01:27,880
No, not all at once.

40
00:01:27,922 --> 00:01:29,256
Then how?

41
00:01:29,297 --> 00:01:30,549
Leonard.

42
00:01:32,259 --> 00:01:33,468
Now, Raj.

43
00:01:35,637 --> 00:01:36,889
Now, Sheldon.

44
00:01:39,391 --> 00:01:40,726
I didn't get a good look.
Can I go again?

45
00:01:40,768 --> 00:01:42,352
No.

46
00:01:42,394 --> 00:01:44,103
It's David
Underhill. So what?

47
00:01:44,145 --> 00:01:45,355
SHELDON
So what?

48
00:01:45,397 --> 00:01:47,608
His observation
of high-energy positrons

49
00:01:47,649 --> 00:01:49,317
has provided the first
conclusive evidence

50
00:01:49,359 --> 00:01:51,070
for the existence
of galactic dark matter.

51
00:01:51,110 --> 00:01:52,571
I have two words for you.

52
00:01:52,613 --> 00:01:54,990
The first is "big,"
the other's "whoop."

53
00:01:55,032 --> 00:01:55,991
It is a big whoop.

54
00:01:56,033 --> 00:01:57,701
It made almost all the work

55
00:01:57,743 --> 00:01:59,327
you've done since you've been
here completely useless.

56
00:01:59,369 --> 00:02:00,495
Did not.
Did, too.

57
00:02:00,537 --> 00:02:01,705
Did...

58
00:02:01,747 --> 00:02:04,291
Okay, maybe
some of it, but...

59
00:02:04,332 --> 00:02:05,918
Look, the guy was just
in the right place,

60
00:02:05,960 --> 00:02:08,045
at the right time with
the right paradigm-shifting

61
00:02:08,087 --> 00:02:11,673
reinterpretation of the
universe. He got lucky.

62
00:02:11,715 --> 00:02:13,592
In more ways than one.

63
00:02:13,634 --> 00:02:16,344
He's a very handsome man.

64
00:02:16,386 --> 00:02:18,555
Doesn't do anything for me.
If I was gonna go that way,

65
00:02:18,597 --> 00:02:21,976
I'm more of a Zac
Ephron kinda guy.

66
00:02:24,019 --> 00:02:26,438
Oh, yeah, like you have
a shot with Zac Ephron.

67
00:02:27,815 --> 00:02:28,774
Excuse me...

68
00:02:28,816 --> 00:02:30,609
Are you Leonard Hofstadter?

69
00:02:30,651 --> 00:02:31,986
Uh... yeah.

70
00:02:32,027 --> 00:02:33,236
I'm David Underhill.

71
00:02:33,278 --> 00:02:36,364
Uh... y-yeah.

72
00:02:36,406 --> 00:02:37,950
Yeah, Dr. Gablehauser
said if I wanted

73
00:02:37,992 --> 00:02:39,910
to set something up
in the photomultiplier lab,

74
00:02:39,952 --> 00:02:41,870
that you'd be able
to give me a hand?

75
00:02:41,912 --> 00:02:43,246
You want to work with me? 
Well,

76
00:02:43,288 --> 00:02:44,748
if you have a little time, yeah.

77
00:02:44,790 --> 00:02:47,126
Wow.... Y-yeah, sure.

78
00:02:47,167 --> 00:02:49,086
Yeah, no problem. Uh...

79
00:02:49,128 --> 00:02:50,587
Here's my home number.

80
00:02:50,629 --> 00:02:51,463
Here's my cell.

81
00:02:51,505 --> 00:02:53,465
Here's my office.

82
00:02:53,507 --> 00:02:56,551
Here's my parents' number
up in New Jersey.

83
00:02:56,593 --> 00:02:58,512
They always know how
to reach me. So...
Okay.

84
00:02:58,553 --> 00:03:01,556
Congratulations on the MacArthur
Grant, by the way. Big fan.

85
00:03:01,598 --> 00:03:03,600
Thanks. I'll call you.

86
00:03:03,642 --> 00:03:05,352
Okay.

87
00:03:05,393 --> 00:03:07,354
Bye-bye!

88
00:03:10,941 --> 00:03:12,442
What are you looking at?

89
00:03:12,484 --> 00:03:13,819
You've never seen
a hypocrite before?

90
00:03:15,612 --> 00:03:19,033
♪ Our whole universe
was in a hot, dense state ♪

91
00:03:19,074 --> 00:03:22,201
♪ Then nearly 14 billion years
ago expansion started... Wait! ♪

92
00:03:22,243 --> 00:03:24,163
♪ The Earth began to cool ♪

93
00:03:24,203 --> 00:03:26,665
♪ The autotrophs began to drool,
Neanderthals developed tools ♪

94
00:03:26,707 --> 00:03:29,292
♪ We built the Wall ♪
♪ We built the pyramids ♪

95
00:03:29,334 --> 00:03:32,004
♪ Math, Science, History,
unraveling the mystery ♪

96
00:03:32,046 --> 00:03:33,797
♪ That all started
with a big bang ♪

97
00:03:33,839 --> 00:03:35,632
♪ Bang! ♪

98
00:03:39,386 --> 00:03:40,971
Trailing badly,
Wolowitz needs a strike

99
00:03:41,013 --> 00:03:43,222
if he has any hopes of
catching up with Sheldon Cooper,

100
00:03:43,264 --> 00:03:45,017
who is dominating
in the ninth frame

101
00:03:45,059 --> 00:03:48,562
with a career-best 68.

102
00:03:56,862 --> 00:03:59,031
LEONARD:
Hey, guys!

103
00:03:59,073 --> 00:04:00,157
WOLOWITZ:
That doesn't count.

104
00:04:00,199 --> 00:04:01,616
Do over! Do over!

105
00:04:01,658 --> 00:04:05,079
There are no do-overs
in Wii bowling.

106
00:04:05,120 --> 00:04:09,666
There are always do-overs
when my people play sports.

107
00:04:09,708 --> 00:04:10,959
Where were you
that's more important

108
00:04:11,001 --> 00:04:11,960
than Wii bowling night?

109
00:04:12,002 --> 00:04:13,045
Actually, I was...

110
00:04:13,087 --> 00:04:14,171
It's a rhetorical question.

111
00:04:14,213 --> 00:04:15,047
There is nothing more important

112
00:04:15,089 --> 00:04:16,798
than Wii bowling night.

113
00:04:16,840 --> 00:04:19,093
Come on, it's just a video game.

114
00:04:19,134 --> 00:04:19,718
And we suck at it.

115
00:04:19,760 --> 00:04:21,386
(gasps)

116
00:04:21,428 --> 00:04:25,140
Nice motivational speech
from the team captain.

117
00:04:25,182 --> 00:04:26,433
Where were you?

118
00:04:26,474 --> 00:04:28,227
I was working
with Dave Underhill.

119
00:04:28,268 --> 00:04:33,023
Ooh, "Dave." Sounds like
Leonard's got a new BFF.

120
00:04:33,065 --> 00:04:34,649
Actually, he's pretty cool.

121
00:04:34,691 --> 00:04:37,027
I mean, not only is he
a brilliant scientist,

122
00:04:37,069 --> 00:04:39,404
but it turns out he's
a Black Diamond skier.

123
00:04:39,446 --> 00:04:43,117
He collects vintage motorcycles.
He plays in a rock band.

124
00:04:43,158 --> 00:04:45,160
So? We're in
a rock band.

125
00:04:45,202 --> 00:04:47,037
No...

126
00:04:47,079 --> 00:04:50,624
We play Rock Band on our X-Box.

127
00:04:50,665 --> 00:04:54,086
Nice motivational speech
from our lead guitarist.

128
00:04:54,128 --> 00:04:55,336
He's funny, too.

129
00:04:55,378 --> 00:04:57,338
He does this
hysterical impersonation

130
00:04:57,380 --> 00:04:59,466
of Stephen Hawking
having phone sex.

131
00:04:59,507 --> 00:05:00,759
(laughing)

132
00:05:00,801 --> 00:05:03,344
(imitating):
What are you wearing?

133
00:05:04,679 --> 00:05:07,182
That's not...
He does it better.

134
00:05:07,224 --> 00:05:09,059
Anyway, he said he was gonna
take me to the gym tomorrow,

135
00:05:09,101 --> 00:05:10,769
so I'm gonna go
practice my situps.

136
00:05:12,562 --> 00:05:16,191
Whoa. Humongous man crush, dude.

137
00:05:16,233 --> 00:05:20,361
Yeah. It's officially
a bro-mance.

138
00:05:20,403 --> 00:05:21,613
Hey, Sheldon,
are you and Leonard

139
00:05:21,655 --> 00:05:22,781
putting up a Christmas tree?

140
00:05:22,823 --> 00:05:24,365
No, because we don't celebrate

141
00:05:24,407 --> 00:05:27,119
the ancient pagan festival
of Saturnalia.

142
00:05:28,411 --> 00:05:29,412
Saturnalia?

143
00:05:29,454 --> 00:05:31,081
Gather round, kids,

144
00:05:31,123 --> 00:05:36,128
it's time for Sheldon's
beloved Christmas special.

145
00:05:36,170 --> 00:05:37,754
In the pre-Christian era,

146
00:05:37,796 --> 00:05:40,299
as the winter solstice
approached and the plants died,

147
00:05:40,339 --> 00:05:42,759
pagans brought evergreen boughs
into their homes

148
00:05:42,801 --> 00:05:44,803
as an act of sympathetic
magic, intended

149
00:05:44,845 --> 00:05:47,681
to guard the life essences
of the plants until spring.

150
00:05:47,722 --> 00:05:49,390
This custom was
later appropriated

151
00:05:49,432 --> 00:05:51,392
by Northern Europeans
and eventually it becomes

152
00:05:51,434 --> 00:05:53,478
the so-called Christmas tree.

153
00:05:55,480 --> 00:05:59,442
And that, Charlie Brown, is
what boredom is all about.

154
00:06:00,902 --> 00:06:02,112
Okay, well,
thank you

155
00:06:02,154 --> 00:06:03,655
for that, but I got
you and Leonard

156
00:06:03,697 --> 00:06:05,324
a few silly neighbor gifts,
so I'll just

157
00:06:05,364 --> 00:06:05,991
put them under my tree.

158
00:06:06,033 --> 00:06:07,868
Wait!

159
00:06:07,909 --> 00:06:09,203
You bought me a present?

160
00:06:09,244 --> 00:06:10,287
Uh-huh.

161
00:06:10,329 --> 00:06:12,873
Why would you do such a thing?

162
00:06:12,914 --> 00:06:14,791
I don't know.
'Cause it's Christmas?

163
00:06:14,833 --> 00:06:17,627
Oh, Penny. I know you think
you're being generous,

164
00:06:17,669 --> 00:06:20,463
but the foundation
of gift-giving is reciprocity.

165
00:06:20,505 --> 00:06:22,132
You haven't given me a gift.

166
00:06:22,174 --> 00:06:24,218
You've given me an obligation.

167
00:06:25,927 --> 00:06:28,722
Don't feel bad, Penny, it's
a classic rookie mistake.

168
00:06:28,763 --> 00:06:30,224
My first Hanukah
with Sheldon,

169
00:06:30,265 --> 00:06:32,059
he yelled at me
for eight nights.

170
00:06:34,102 --> 00:06:36,313
Now, hey, it's okay.
You don't have to get
me anything in return.

171
00:06:36,355 --> 00:06:38,773
Of course I do.
The essence of the custom

172
00:06:38,815 --> 00:06:40,399
is that I now have to go out
and purchase for you

173
00:06:40,441 --> 00:06:42,152
a gift of commensurate value

174
00:06:42,194 --> 00:06:44,321
and representing the same
perceived level of friendship

175
00:06:44,363 --> 00:06:47,908
as that represented
by the gift you've given me.

176
00:06:47,949 --> 00:06:51,912
It's no wonder suicide rates
skyrocket this time of year.

177
00:06:51,953 --> 00:06:52,954
Okay, you know what? Forget it.

178
00:06:52,996 --> 00:06:54,497
I'm not giving you a present.

179
00:06:54,539 --> 00:06:56,250
No, it's too late. I see it.
That elf sticker

180
00:06:56,291 --> 00:06:57,542
says, "To Sheldon."

181
00:06:58,543 --> 00:07:00,003
The die has been cast.

182
00:07:00,045 --> 00:07:01,504
The moving finger has writ

183
00:07:01,546 --> 00:07:04,507
Hannibal has crossed the alps.

184
00:07:04,549 --> 00:07:05,342
(whispering)
(laughing)

185
00:07:05,384 --> 00:07:06,634
I know. It's funny

186
00:07:06,676 --> 00:07:08,803
when it's not
happening to us.

187
00:07:10,555 --> 00:07:13,267
Sheldon, I am very, very sorry.

188
00:07:13,308 --> 00:07:15,143
No. No, I brought this on myself

189
00:07:15,185 --> 00:07:18,397
by being such an endearing
and important part of your life.

190
00:07:19,856 --> 00:07:22,317
I'm going to need
a ride to the mall.

191
00:07:23,193 --> 00:07:25,445
It's happening to us.

192
00:07:27,030 --> 00:07:27,989
(thud)
Ow!

193
00:07:28,031 --> 00:07:30,158
Ha-ha-ha. Ow.

194
00:07:30,200 --> 00:07:31,034
Are you gonna make it?

195
00:07:31,076 --> 00:07:32,077
Yeah, I guess.

196
00:07:32,119 --> 00:07:34,413
All right.

197
00:07:34,454 --> 00:07:38,125
Thanks for letting me
try out your motorcycle.

198
00:07:38,166 --> 00:07:39,459
No problem.

199
00:07:39,500 --> 00:07:42,712
I had no idea
it was so heavy.

200
00:07:42,754 --> 00:07:45,340
The thing just fell right
over on me, didn't it?

201
00:07:45,382 --> 00:07:48,802
Yeah. Lucky for you
it wasn't moving.

202
00:07:48,843 --> 00:07:50,929
Oh, hey, Leonard.
Ooh, are you okay?
Oh, yeah.

203
00:07:50,971 --> 00:07:53,556
It's just a little
motorcycle accident.

204
00:07:53,598 --> 00:07:55,392
My God, how fast were you going?

205
00:07:55,434 --> 00:07:57,936
I don't know. It's
all such a blur.

206
00:07:57,978 --> 00:07:59,604
(laughing)

207
00:07:59,646 --> 00:08:01,606
Good one. He couldn't
even get it started.

208
00:08:01,648 --> 00:08:02,441
(laughing)

209
00:08:02,482 --> 00:08:04,025
Hi. Dave.
Hi.

210
00:08:04,067 --> 00:08:06,194
Penny. So it's your motorcycle?

211
00:08:06,236 --> 00:08:06,945
Uh-huh.
Ohh...

212
00:08:06,987 --> 00:08:08,029
Is it okay?

213
00:08:08,071 --> 00:08:09,823
It's fine.

214
00:08:09,864 --> 00:08:10,657
Lucky for the bike

215
00:08:10,698 --> 00:08:13,701
it landed on my leg.

216
00:08:13,743 --> 00:08:15,245
You mind giving me a hand
with speed racer here?

217
00:08:15,287 --> 00:08:16,537
Oh, yeah. Yeah, sure.

218
00:08:16,579 --> 00:08:19,040
So, um, Dave, how
do you know Leonard?

219
00:08:19,082 --> 00:08:20,292
I'm a physicist.

220
00:08:20,334 --> 00:08:23,711
(laughing)
No, you're not.

221
00:08:23,753 --> 00:08:25,339
Why is that
so surprising?

222
00:08:25,380 --> 00:08:27,882
Uh, well, it's just that
the physicists I know

223
00:08:27,924 --> 00:08:31,510
are indoorsy and pale.

224
00:08:31,552 --> 00:08:32,887
I'm not indoorsy.

225
00:08:32,929 --> 00:08:34,722
I just wear
the appropriate sun block

226
00:08:34,764 --> 00:08:36,683
because I don't take
melanoma lightly.

227
00:08:36,724 --> 00:08:38,226
So, are you

228
00:08:38,268 --> 00:08:39,519
and Leonard working on
an experiment together?

229
00:08:39,560 --> 00:08:41,062
Yeah,
actually we are.

230
00:08:41,104 --> 00:08:42,481
Yeah, we're examining
the radiation levels

231
00:08:42,563 --> 00:08:43,731
of photomultiplier tubes

232
00:08:43,773 --> 00:08:45,108
for a new
dark matter detector.

233
00:08:45,150 --> 00:08:46,234
Uh, sweetie, sweetie,

234
00:08:46,276 --> 00:08:48,653
Dave was talking.

235
00:08:48,695 --> 00:08:50,780
You know, I love science.

236
00:08:50,822 --> 00:08:53,367
Since when?
Since always.

237
00:08:53,408 --> 00:08:55,576
Call me a geek,
but I am just nuts

238
00:08:55,618 --> 00:08:58,579
for the whole subatomic-
particle thing.

239
00:08:58,621 --> 00:09:00,874
The last thing I would
ever call you is a geek.

240
00:09:00,915 --> 00:09:02,834
(laughs) Ha. Well,
that's what I am--

241
00:09:02,876 --> 00:09:04,669
queen of the nerds.

242
00:09:04,711 --> 00:09:06,046
DAVE:
Well, if you like,

243
00:09:06,087 --> 00:09:08,006
I could show you the
lab we're working in.

244
00:09:08,048 --> 00:09:10,591
We've got some cool toys,
you know-- lasers and stuff.

245
00:09:10,633 --> 00:09:13,928
You know, I have always wanted
to see a big science lab.

246
00:09:13,970 --> 00:09:14,929
Since when?

247
00:09:14,971 --> 00:09:16,931
Since always.

248
00:09:16,973 --> 00:09:18,599
Leonard, are you
okay here?
Uh...

249
00:09:18,641 --> 00:09:20,852
Yeah. I guess.
How 'bout we go
see it now?

250
00:09:20,894 --> 00:09:22,603
Maybe afterwards we take
the bike up the coast,

251
00:09:22,645 --> 00:09:24,064
We grab a little
bite to eat...

252
00:09:24,105 --> 00:09:25,899
Um... Yeah. Yeah,
that sounds great.

253
00:09:25,940 --> 00:09:27,025
Let me just
get my jacket.

254
00:09:30,778 --> 00:09:31,612
(door closes)

255
00:09:31,654 --> 00:09:34,908
Boy, she'll do, huh?

256
00:09:34,949 --> 00:09:37,536
Yeah, if you
like that type.

257
00:09:37,577 --> 00:09:39,662
So, you and her...?

258
00:09:39,704 --> 00:09:40,663
No, just neighbors.

259
00:09:40,705 --> 00:09:42,249
Really.

260
00:09:42,290 --> 00:09:43,583
I don't know how you live
next door to that

261
00:09:43,624 --> 00:09:46,002
without doing
something about it.

262
00:09:47,170 --> 00:09:49,297
Actually...

263
00:09:49,339 --> 00:09:50,965
science is my lady.

264
00:09:53,593 --> 00:09:54,553
Okay. Let's go.

265
00:09:54,593 --> 00:09:55,554
All right.

266
00:09:55,595 --> 00:09:56,804
See you tomorrow,
Leonard.

267
00:09:56,846 --> 00:09:59,224
See ya. 'Bye, Penny.

268
00:09:59,266 --> 00:10:00,517
Have fun.

269
00:10:02,602 --> 00:10:03,770
(thuds against door)

270
00:10:04,979 --> 00:10:06,189
Yes?

271
00:10:13,947 --> 00:10:16,032
Did you forget your key?

272
00:10:19,244 --> 00:10:21,996
(Christmas music playing)

273
00:10:26,667 --> 00:10:30,630
I don't see anything
in here a woman would want.

274
00:10:30,671 --> 00:10:32,090
You're kidding.

275
00:10:32,132 --> 00:10:35,218
You've got lotions
and bath oils and soaps.

276
00:10:35,260 --> 00:10:39,639
I mean, that's the
estrogen hat trick.

277
00:10:39,680 --> 00:10:41,891
What it is
is a cacophonous assault

278
00:10:41,933 --> 00:10:46,020
of eucalyptus, bayberry,
cinnamon and vanilla.

279
00:10:46,062 --> 00:10:49,857
It's as if my head were trapped
in the pajamas of a sultan.

280
00:10:49,899 --> 00:10:51,401
Sheldon, if you don't
like this stuff,

281
00:10:51,443 --> 00:10:54,904
let's just go next door
and build her a bear.

282
00:10:54,946 --> 00:10:59,075
I told you before,
bears are terrifying.

283
00:10:59,117 --> 00:11:00,285
WOLOWITZ:
Come on,

284
00:11:00,327 --> 00:11:02,120
bath stuff.
It's perfect!

285
00:11:02,162 --> 00:11:05,039
You got a scented candle,
a cleansing buff,

286
00:11:05,081 --> 00:11:07,875
spearmint and green tea scented
bath oil, promotes relaxation.

287
00:11:07,917 --> 00:11:09,586
That presupposes
Penny is tense.

288
00:11:09,628 --> 00:11:10,795
She knows you.
She's tense.

289
00:11:10,837 --> 00:11:12,631
We all are.
Buy a basket!

290
00:11:12,671 --> 00:11:13,840
Excuse me, we're ready.

291
00:11:13,881 --> 00:11:15,674
No, we're not.

292
00:11:15,716 --> 00:11:17,511
Let's say for a moment
that I accept

293
00:11:17,552 --> 00:11:19,387
the bath item gift hypothesis,

294
00:11:19,429 --> 00:11:21,473
I now lay the following
conundrum at your feet:

295
00:11:21,515 --> 00:11:24,017
Which size?

296
00:11:24,058 --> 00:11:25,560
This one.
Let's go.

297
00:11:25,602 --> 00:11:27,437
You put no thought into that.

298
00:11:27,479 --> 00:11:29,939
I'm sorry.
Uh... this one.

299
00:11:29,981 --> 00:11:32,775
Let's go!

300
00:11:32,817 --> 00:11:34,819
I have insufficient
data to proceed.

301
00:11:34,861 --> 00:11:35,945
Excuse me, miss?

302
00:11:35,987 --> 00:11:37,822
Yes?

303
00:11:37,864 --> 00:11:39,949
If I were to give you
this gift basket,

304
00:11:39,991 --> 00:11:42,494
based on that action alone
and no other data,

305
00:11:42,536 --> 00:11:45,288
infer and describe
the hypothetical relationship

306
00:11:45,330 --> 00:11:48,916
that exists between us.

307
00:11:48,958 --> 00:11:51,252
Excuse me?

308
00:11:51,294 --> 00:11:53,129
Here.

309
00:11:53,171 --> 00:11:54,506
Now, are we friends?

310
00:11:54,548 --> 00:11:57,925
Colleagues? Lovers?

311
00:11:57,967 --> 00:12:00,136
Are you my grandmother?

312
00:12:01,638 --> 00:12:03,431
I don't understand what
you're talking about

313
00:12:03,473 --> 00:12:06,434
and you're making me
a little uncomfortable.

314
00:12:06,476 --> 00:12:08,061
See? Sounds just
like you and Penny.

315
00:12:08,102 --> 00:12:09,145
We'll take it.

316
00:12:12,691 --> 00:12:14,150
(laughing)

317
00:12:14,192 --> 00:12:16,277
Hey, Leonard.
Come, join us.

318
00:12:16,319 --> 00:12:17,278
Oh, hey, Dave.

319
00:12:17,320 --> 00:12:19,614
And Penny, what a surprise.

320
00:12:19,656 --> 00:12:21,074
Hey, Leonard.

321
00:12:21,115 --> 00:12:22,576
Dave was just showing me
around the university.

322
00:12:22,617 --> 00:12:23,243
You know,
this place

323
00:12:23,284 --> 00:12:24,578
is unbelievable!

324
00:12:24,619 --> 00:12:25,995
Yeah, I know.
I've been offering

325
00:12:26,037 --> 00:12:28,831
to show you around
for a year and a half.

326
00:12:28,873 --> 00:12:31,000
You always said you had yoga.

327
00:12:31,042 --> 00:12:32,293
I never said that.

328
00:12:32,335 --> 00:12:34,170
Maybe I heard you wrong.

329
00:12:34,212 --> 00:12:37,298
A lot of words
sound like "yoga."

330
00:12:37,340 --> 00:12:39,092
This is an amazing
woman, Leonard.

331
00:12:39,133 --> 00:12:41,135
She has a curious
and agile mind,

332
00:12:41,177 --> 00:12:44,180
not to mention being curious
and agile in other respects.

333
00:12:44,222 --> 00:12:46,182
(laughing):
Oh, shut up!

334
00:12:46,224 --> 00:12:49,686
Yes, please shut up.

335
00:12:49,728 --> 00:12:51,479
So, um, Dave,

336
00:12:51,521 --> 00:12:53,856
don't you think you and I
should get back to the lab?

337
00:12:53,898 --> 00:12:56,234
You know, that dark matter
isn't going to detect itself.

338
00:13:00,280 --> 00:13:02,282
Actually, I was thinking about
taking the afternoon off

339
00:13:02,323 --> 00:13:04,825
so I could work on another
experiment with Penny.

340
00:13:04,867 --> 00:13:06,661
Really? We're going
to do an experiment?

341
00:13:06,703 --> 00:13:09,122
Uh-huh. We're going to explore
the effects of tequila shots

342
00:13:09,163 --> 00:13:11,790
on a gorgeous
22-year-old woman.

343
00:13:11,832 --> 00:13:13,293
It's not an experiment!

344
00:13:13,334 --> 00:13:15,295
You saw what happened
last night. (laughing)

345
00:13:16,795 --> 00:13:18,506
You ready to go?
Yeah.

346
00:13:18,548 --> 00:13:19,466
Oh, can I drive
the motorcycle?

347
00:13:19,507 --> 00:13:20,799
Yeah, why not?

348
00:13:20,841 --> 00:13:23,886
You can't do any
worse than Leonard.

349
00:13:23,928 --> 00:13:25,555
That's funny.

350
00:13:27,890 --> 00:13:29,434
By the way, my leg
is killing me.

351
00:13:29,476 --> 00:13:32,019
Thanks for asking.

352
00:13:53,874 --> 00:13:56,294
Mmm, great news, Leonard.

353
00:13:56,336 --> 00:13:58,713
I've solved my
Penny gift dilemma.

354
00:13:58,755 --> 00:14:00,715
Yippee.

355
00:14:00,757 --> 00:14:04,135
You see, the danger was that I
might under or over-reciprocate,

356
00:14:04,177 --> 00:14:05,970
but I have devised
a foolproof plan.

357
00:14:06,012 --> 00:14:08,389
See, I will open
her gift to me first

358
00:14:08,431 --> 00:14:10,141
and then excuse myself,

359
00:14:10,183 --> 00:14:11,892
feigning digestive distress.

360
00:14:11,934 --> 00:14:15,146
Then I'll look up the
price of her gift online,

361
00:14:15,188 --> 00:14:17,357
choose the basket
closest to that value,

362
00:14:17,398 --> 00:14:19,025
give it to her

363
00:14:19,066 --> 00:14:21,944
and then I'll return the
others for a full refund.

364
00:14:23,404 --> 00:14:25,198
Brilliant.

365
00:14:25,239 --> 00:14:27,701
It is, isn't it?

366
00:14:27,742 --> 00:14:29,910
Is it okay if I hide
them in your room?

367
00:14:29,952 --> 00:14:33,373
The smell makes
me nauseated.

368
00:14:33,414 --> 00:14:34,666
Do whatever you want.

369
00:14:34,708 --> 00:14:36,501
Thank you, that's very gracious.

370
00:14:38,419 --> 00:14:39,462
Gentlemen.

371
00:14:43,758 --> 00:14:45,760
Why couldn't you have just
done what Leonard did

372
00:14:45,802 --> 00:14:48,887
and get Penny
a new boyfriend?

373
00:14:52,183 --> 00:14:53,476
My leg is killing me.

374
00:14:53,518 --> 00:14:56,103
Thanks for asking.

375
00:15:03,444 --> 00:15:05,905
(knocking on door)

376
00:15:05,946 --> 00:15:09,367
Okay, I have just
one question for you.

377
00:15:09,409 --> 00:15:12,161
While I am perfectly happy with
the way things are between us,

378
00:15:12,203 --> 00:15:13,705
you said that
you didn't want

379
00:15:13,747 --> 00:15:15,248
to go out with me because
I was too smart for you!

380
00:15:15,289 --> 00:15:17,458
Well, news flash, lady:

381
00:15:17,500 --> 00:15:20,127
David Underhill is ten
times smarter than me!

382
00:15:20,169 --> 00:15:22,296
You'd have to drive a
railroad spike into his brain

383
00:15:22,338 --> 00:15:24,382
for me to beat him at checkers!

384
00:15:24,424 --> 00:15:25,966
Next to him,

385
00:15:26,008 --> 00:15:27,926
I'm like one of those
sign-language gorillas

386
00:15:27,968 --> 00:15:31,097
who knows how to ask for grapes!

387
00:15:31,138 --> 00:15:33,683
So, my question is...

388
00:15:33,725 --> 00:15:37,771
What's up with that?!

389
00:15:37,812 --> 00:15:40,022
(crying):
Why are you yelling at me?

390
00:15:40,064 --> 00:15:42,108
Sorry, I'm sorry, I'm sorry!

391
00:15:42,149 --> 00:15:44,694
Never mind, we're cool.

392
00:15:44,736 --> 00:15:46,279
Dave is not smarter than you.

393
00:15:46,320 --> 00:15:48,406
He's an idiot.

394
00:15:48,448 --> 00:15:50,949
Really? Why would you say that?

395
00:15:50,991 --> 00:15:54,245
Because a smart guy takes
the nude photos of his wife

396
00:15:54,287 --> 00:15:56,581
off his cell phone before
he tries to take nude photos

397
00:15:56,623 --> 00:16:00,376
of his girlfriend.

398
00:16:00,418 --> 00:16:02,921
He tried to take
nude photos of you?

399
00:16:02,962 --> 00:16:06,215
That's what you took
from that?!

400
00:16:07,801 --> 00:16:09,803
The guy is married!

401
00:16:09,844 --> 00:16:11,930
Oh, yeah.

402
00:16:11,971 --> 00:16:15,099
I'm so-- Oh, that's terrible.

403
00:16:15,141 --> 00:16:16,643
And you, if you are so okay

404
00:16:16,684 --> 00:16:17,769
with the way things
are between us,

405
00:16:17,811 --> 00:16:19,186
why are you so jealous?

406
00:16:19,228 --> 00:16:20,271
Well, uh...

407
00:16:22,231 --> 00:16:26,235
The important thing is he's
married and that's terrible!

408
00:16:27,987 --> 00:16:30,990
Nice save, genius.

409
00:16:31,031 --> 00:16:32,241
Eggnog?

410
00:16:32,283 --> 00:16:35,035
Lactose.

411
00:16:35,077 --> 00:16:36,412
It's just rum.

412
00:16:36,454 --> 00:16:40,082
It stopped being eggnog
like half an hour ago.

413
00:16:44,170 --> 00:16:47,340
Smooth.

414
00:16:47,381 --> 00:16:50,217
Smoother than you.

415
00:16:50,259 --> 00:16:54,722
Come on, it's Christmas,
just give me this one.

416
00:16:54,764 --> 00:16:58,977
Okay, Merry Christmas.

417
00:17:03,147 --> 00:17:04,732
By the way, my leg
is killing me.

418
00:17:04,774 --> 00:17:08,152
Thanks for asking.

419
00:17:12,156 --> 00:17:15,033
(knocking on door)

420
00:17:15,075 --> 00:17:16,619
Merry Christmas.
Merry Christmas.

421
00:17:16,661 --> 00:17:18,078
How's your leg?

422
00:17:18,120 --> 00:17:19,288
Very good, thanks
for asking. Come on in.

423
00:17:21,248 --> 00:17:25,211
Ah, good, Penny, you're
here to exchange gifts.

424
00:17:27,922 --> 00:17:29,382
You'll be pleased to know

425
00:17:29,423 --> 00:17:32,844
I'm prepared for
whatever you have to offer.

426
00:17:32,886 --> 00:17:34,846
Okay, here.

427
00:17:38,182 --> 00:17:41,728
I should note I'm having
some digestive distress,

428
00:17:43,437 --> 00:17:48,860
so, if I excuse myself
abruptly, don't be alarmed.

429
00:17:55,366 --> 00:18:00,496
Oh, a napkin.

430
00:18:00,538 --> 00:18:02,289
Turn it over.

431
00:18:08,713 --> 00:18:10,339
"To Sheldon,

432
00:18:10,381 --> 00:18:13,885
"live long and prosper.

433
00:18:13,927 --> 00:18:17,179
Leonard Nimoy."

434
00:18:18,973 --> 00:18:20,725
Yeah, he came
into the restaurant.

435
00:18:20,767 --> 00:18:23,143
Sorry the napkin's dirty.
He wiped his mouth with it.

436
00:18:28,566 --> 00:18:32,862
I possess the DNA
of Leonard Nimoy?!

437
00:18:35,949 --> 00:18:38,076
Well...

438
00:18:40,620 --> 00:18:42,038
Yeah, yeah. I guess.

439
00:18:42,080 --> 00:18:43,915
But look, he signed it.

440
00:18:43,957 --> 00:18:47,043
Do you realize
what this means?!

441
00:18:48,628 --> 00:18:50,379
All I need is a healthy ovum

442
00:18:50,421 --> 00:18:55,593
and I can grow
my own Leonard Nimoy!

443
00:18:57,971 --> 00:19:01,933
Okay, all I'm giving you
is the napkin, Sheldon.

444
00:19:03,977 --> 00:19:06,145
Be right back.

445
00:19:08,522 --> 00:19:10,524
Here. Open it.

446
00:19:12,986 --> 00:19:17,949
Oh, a gift certificate
for motorcycle lessons.

447
00:19:19,993 --> 00:19:21,410
Very thoughtful.

448
00:19:21,452 --> 00:19:22,578
Yeah, and I checked.

449
00:19:22,620 --> 00:19:24,038
Not letting the
bike fall on you

450
00:19:24,080 --> 00:19:27,541
while standing still
is lesson one.

451
00:19:27,583 --> 00:19:30,670
Oh, then, I think you'll
appreciate what I got you.

452
00:19:30,711 --> 00:19:33,131
Okay.

453
00:19:35,716 --> 00:19:39,637
"101 Totally Cool Science
Experiments for Kids."

454
00:19:44,350 --> 00:19:47,311
You know, 'cause you're
so into science.

455
00:19:58,238 --> 00:20:00,992
Sheldon!
What did you do?!

456
00:20:01,034 --> 00:20:03,577
I know!

457
00:20:03,619 --> 00:20:05,538
It's not enough, is it?

458
00:20:08,166 --> 00:20:09,834
Here.

459
00:20:22,513 --> 00:20:27,185
Leonard, look!
Sheldon's hugging me.

460
00:20:27,226 --> 00:20:37,235
It's a Saturnalia miracle.

461
00:20:37,277 --> 00:20:47,329
It's a Saturnalia miracle.

462
00:20:47,329 --> 00:21:00,634
It's a Saturnalia miracle.

